{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2502 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f8dfad4c83817750c127f7d2fb01980b852a77dc276bcf74c46e91b48115f236" :edb/db-before-t 2501 :edb/db-after-t 2502 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535814 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:56:45.768Z" 13194139535814 true] [17592186175701 1100 "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/7dad070080562e459c926f5c8fa3d71a56981d68bd3ed4aae768c2f7450685a3.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535814 false] [17592186175701 1100 "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/6e4a720878567b81cb593aa5a9bfc5aa2c8c7ace61d111bb8934bc94056c04bc.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535814 true] [17592186175701 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/6786c7bc4d12bc3ee1a335eeb45967475850a585ec36805fe81aa76360d5a729.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/a73682dbe1dfccc14e5b3f0c68aa6e6aa7eb7a67bb475efbbaeaa1e886585554.png)" 13194139535814 false] [17592186175701 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/0d2e7b9f7011624cf74762419efd784144cf41e2000301d093e1aa612e96f495.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/2e4bc467e9fbc15574d2739766eada3f297500ecf1a69382500271751b2edf65.png)" 13194139535814 true] [17592186175702 1100 "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535814 false] [17592186175702 1100 "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535814 true] [17592186175703 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/e6a0d43572790d4daa49659c271c17032f2601458db0f6843257df07c245f372.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/9bf515d20e5a19646680474f0243dba793d1383eade4bf4986c7b11501baa800.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/1afe535b20d0fd1ce03b2b0886424a5ef1a29f4eea7e47c1aa818af67c98e868.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" 13194139535814 false] [17592186175703 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/07f01e2a202e29fee28a98d386df1d22fd21efcf1ee4d0a6a4cc56f9b637d57c.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/a547975b3524322daec753a8ddd010e50e815307d72bc07933d1e3674b4b462c.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/53de45d6b1504ec53ca81471731b06ac5524a12230f0414553624c116a6f47c6.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" 13194139535814 true] [17592186175703 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/af8f52f23a6fdcd1f070eff5876415cc884ffaafcbb2fe3f8737c31cc4761c86.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" 13194139535814 false] [17592186175703 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/b212192b142e9b4686a4d39e5957db5f3bfbca6283bfcb1d88a6fc57a4a27848.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$" 13194139535814 true] [17592186175852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367826 13194139535814 true] [17592186175853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367812 13194139535814 true] [17592186175853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367819 13194139535814 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367833 13194139535814 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367840 13194139535814 true] [17592186175854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186367847 13194139535814 true] [17592186176397 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535814 true] [17592186176397 1100 "If $v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ =-2\\textrm{i}$,\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$?" 13194139535814 false] [17592186176397 1100 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139535814 true] [17592186176397 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10+2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367812 1017 #edb/ref 17592186367813 13194139535814 true] [17592186367812 1089 "q-140383" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367812 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535814 true] [17592186367812 1100 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367812 1113 #uuid "cfeca104-fe47-5c0d-8612-60fbd83f3f78" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367812 1145 "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(4+3\\text{i}) \\\\ (-1)(4+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4+3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}.$" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367814 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367815 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367816 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367817 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186367818 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1041 "selection" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1104 #edb/ref 17592186367817 13194139535814 true] [17592186367813 1110 #uuid "fd077df7-8220-5b57-82cf-62aead5b0794" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367814 1034 "\\lambda =4-3\\text{i}" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535814 true] [17592186367814 1111 #uuid "494ad08e-b220-5b32-bd03-23643745c2ce" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367815 1034 "\\lambda =3-4\\text{i}" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535814 true] [17592186367815 1111 #uuid "ee79e0e0-6a8c-502c-ac27-a4913d40a7b1" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367816 1034 "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535814 true] [17592186367816 1111 #uuid "8189ed53-deac-56bc-8250-a321f067f001" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367817 1034 "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535814 true] [17592186367817 1111 #uuid "0cbb9d12-4ae9-5a48-a17b-3a75f45dcfe8" 13194139535814 true] [17592186367818 1034 "\\lambda =3+4\\text{i}" 13194139